1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,380
By the hundred-and-twentieth night, the sea
came up Market Street to meet it.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,080
The first night, we just
thought it was beautiful.

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,670
I was ten. This is what the next
two hundred days were like.

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:17,255
My Nana wrote down the high
tide twice a day.

5
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:20,170
So had her mother. And hers.

6
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:24,410
Forty centimetres over the
table. That's not right.

7
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:27,270
Spring tide, Odile! Put your wellies on!

8
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,855
Not in my book it isn't.

9
00:00:30,225 --> 00:00:33,890
In our kitchen, Mum's pager went
off before the toast did.

10
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:36,325
She drove the lifeboat.

11
00:00:36,695 --> 00:00:40,460
Nana, why do you still do the book?
The harbour has a computer.

12
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:45,400
The computer wasn't here in eighteen
eighty-five. The book was.

13
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:47,810
Nana, look. It's bigger.

14
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:49,925
Yes. It is.

15
00:00:50,295 --> 00:00:55,170
NEWS ANCHOR: Observatories measure the Moon's
distance every night, with lasers. Tonight it is

16
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:58,360
thirty-two thousand kilometres
closer than yesterday.

17
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:01,095
Nobody can yet say why.

18
00:01:01,465 --> 00:01:04,705
That night, Nana wrote in the
book for a long time.

19
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:07,465
Then she locked it in the bread bin.

20
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:10,950
On Day 3 she added a column of her own.

21
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,510
A small tide that came when
the Moon wasn't there.

22
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,720
A closer Moon pulls harder on the sea.

23
00:01:18,090 --> 00:01:21,705
Come half as close, and the tide
is eight times higher.

24
00:01:22,075 --> 00:01:26,040
On Day 9, Nana did sums until
the kettle boiled dry.

25
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:28,525
Nana? It's four in the morning.

26
00:01:28,895 --> 00:01:31,965
Go back to bed, love. I'm nearly done.

27
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:38,405
It is a high spring tide, and it will
settle. The Board sees no cause for alarm.

28
00:01:38,775 --> 00:01:42,520
It won't settle, Arthur. It
will come up Chapel Hill.

29
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:45,655
We've heard your floods before, Odile.

30
00:01:46,025 --> 00:01:49,395
They laughed. Nana put on
her hat, and we left.

31
00:01:49,765 --> 00:01:53,255
On Day 12, I found a paper in Nana's coat.

32
00:01:53,625 --> 00:01:56,815
You bought the chapel? Nana,
it hasn’t got a roof!

33
00:01:57,185 --> 00:01:59,125
It's got a good view.

34
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:03,210
By Day 30, the sea came over
the quay every night.

35
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:07,395
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists now expect the
Moon to settle within the week.

36
00:02:07,765 --> 00:02:12,060
It didn't. Every promise on the
news lasted about four days.

37
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:13,840
Where's my bed?

38
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:16,575
Up the hill. Airing.

39
00:02:16,945 --> 00:02:19,760
I'm not moving, Nana. This is my house.

40
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:22,320
I know it is, love.

41
00:02:22,690 --> 00:02:24,905
Even the calendar went wrong.

42
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:30,290
A closer Moon goes round faster: the
month was down to twenty-two days.

43
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:33,850
By Day 60 the tides had doubled.

44
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,360
Quay Street was only ours at low water.

45
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:42,420
And at low tide the sea went out so far,
we could see the harbour floor.

46
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:45,970
Crabs, bicycles, and somebody's piano.

47
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:49,805
Mum's lifeboat went out
eleven times that week.

48
00:02:50,175 --> 00:02:53,440
Mostly for people who had gone
back for one more thing.

49
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:55,420
What's the line for?

50
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:57,250
For later.

51
00:02:57,810 --> 00:03:02,400
On Day 70, a scientist said the
thing nobody had thought of.

52
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:08,719
Nothing falls this slowly. The Moon isn't
falling. Something is taking its speed

53
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:10,875
away, a little on every loop.

54
00:03:11,245 --> 00:03:16,635
Spacecraft do it on purpose: swing past a
planet, and steal a little of its speed.

55
00:03:17,005 --> 00:03:19,520
Something was doing it to our Moon.

56
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:25,834
We can't see it, because it is a black hole
smaller than a grain of sand, and a

57
00:03:25,834 --> 00:03:27,865
third as heavy as the Moon.

58
00:03:28,235 --> 00:03:30,700
No telescope could find it.

59
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:35,275
The only things big enough to feel
it were the Moon and the sea.

60
00:03:35,645 --> 00:03:38,685
Nana! A tide with no Moon.
That's your column!

61
00:03:39,055 --> 00:03:40,040
Is it.

62
00:03:41,055 --> 00:03:43,045
She didn't even look up.

63
00:03:43,415 --> 00:03:44,955
Day 90.

64
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:50,620
The lighthouse was an island twice a day,
and the school moved up the hill.

65
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:57,174
It wasn't just us. Every port on Earth closed.
Whole cities moved inland, one street

66
00:03:57,174 --> 00:03:57,910
at a time.

67
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:03,841
In some bays the shape of the coast made it
worse. Places that already had big tides

68
00:04:03,841 --> 00:04:04,900
got the biggest.

69
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:07,260
Mum? Is it going to hit us?

70
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:10,325
No, love. I promise you it can't.

71
00:04:10,695 --> 00:04:15,435
Mum was right. A moon doesn't fall onto
its planet. It goes round it.

72
00:04:15,805 --> 00:04:20,552
And one that came really close would be
pulled to pieces long before it touched.

73
00:04:20,552 --> 00:04:23,400
Ours was never going to come
anywhere near that.

74
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,580
You care more about that book
than about our house!

75
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:30,815
The book is how I know what's
coming for our house, love.

76
00:04:31,185 --> 00:04:34,025
I didn't speak to her for three days.

77
00:04:34,395 --> 00:04:38,735
On Day 110, I found two pages
she'd glued together.

78
00:04:39,105 --> 00:04:41,120
Her sum from Day 9.

79
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:45,555
And at the bottom, the last high
tide: thirty-five metres.

80
00:04:45,925 --> 00:04:51,019
And a letter from nineteen ninety-eight. She
had warned them about a flood once

81
00:04:51,019 --> 00:04:51,470
before.

82
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:55,130
They'd written back that there
was no cause for alarm.

83
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,415
You knew. Since Day 9. Why
didn't you tell them?

84
00:04:58,785 --> 00:05:01,805
I did, love. Day 10. You were there.

85
00:05:02,175 --> 00:05:03,335
They laughed.

86
00:05:04,175 --> 00:05:08,932
They'll believe the sea before they believe
me. So I'm getting the hill ready for

87
00:05:08,932 --> 00:05:09,695
when they do.

88
00:05:10,065 --> 00:05:13,905
On Day 120, the sea came up Market Street.

89
00:05:14,275 --> 00:05:17,815
From the steps, I watched it
reach my bedroom window.

90
00:05:18,185 --> 00:05:21,000
Then I couldn't see my window any more.

91
00:05:21,370 --> 00:05:24,485
The next day, the sea came
into the Harbour Board.

92
00:05:24,855 --> 00:05:26,370
It will settle.

93
00:05:26,855 --> 00:05:29,400
He still said it. Just quieter.

94
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:34,135
Even the ground rose and fell now.
Two metres, twice a day.

95
00:05:34,505 --> 00:05:37,420
Nobody felt it. Only the cups did.

96
00:05:37,790 --> 00:05:42,905
Mr. Pell. This is my Nana's book. Every
tide for a hundred and forty years.

97
00:05:43,275 --> 00:05:45,180
Wren, not now.

98
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:50,490
Nana wrote down tonight's tide on Day 9.
Fifteen metres. Go and look at it.

99
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:53,625
Good Lord. She had it right.

100
00:05:53,995 --> 00:05:55,910
She had all of it right.

101
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:58,250
Odile. Where does it stop?

102
00:05:58,620 --> 00:05:59,435
There.

103
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:02,685
Then that's where we go.

104
00:06:03,055 --> 00:06:06,445
Four hundred people slept in
the chapel that night.

105
00:06:06,815 --> 00:06:09,105
Mr. Pell carried the beds.

106
00:06:09,475 --> 00:06:14,715
On Day 160, the scientists finally
had a number of their own.

107
00:06:15,085 --> 00:06:21,645
The visitor gets faster with every loop.
Around Day 200, it will be fast enough to

108
00:06:21,645 --> 00:06:22,205
escape.

109
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:27,635
And the Moon should stop at about a hundred
and twenty-eight thousand kilometres.

110
00:06:28,005 --> 00:06:29,940
Nana. That's your number.

111
00:06:30,310 --> 00:06:31,925
I rounded up.

112
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:38,403
By Day 180, the sea was at the churchyard
wall, and the Moon was nearly three times

113
00:06:38,403 --> 00:06:38,990
as wide.

114
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:44,542
Mum launched the lifeboat one last time, from
a ramp cut into the hill, and brought

115
00:06:44,542 --> 00:06:46,665
a family back from the lighthouse.

116
00:06:47,035 --> 00:06:50,375
That night I saw Nana
writing in the book again.

117
00:06:50,745 --> 00:06:55,285
Not the tide. Just our names.
All four hundred of them.

118
00:06:55,655 --> 00:06:57,970
Nana? What if your sum is wrong?

119
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:02,960
Then we go up one more step, love.
And I buy a better pencil.

120
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:05,095
Day 200.

121
00:07:05,465 --> 00:07:09,430
The whole town stood on the chapel
steps and watched it come.

122
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,140
Two steps below the line. One.

123
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:17,225
It touched the white line. And it stopped.

124
00:07:17,595 --> 00:07:22,925
That night, the visitor made its last loop.
It had stolen all the speed it needed,

125
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:24,875
and it flew off into the dark.

126
00:07:25,245 --> 00:07:30,617
We thought it would take the Moon with it. It
didn't. It left the Moon exactly where

127
00:07:30,617 --> 00:07:31,065
it was.

128
00:07:31,435 --> 00:07:34,130
Your book now, love. Page one.

129
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:36,940
Day 1. High tide: on time.

130
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:40,150
We built the town again, above the line.

131
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:45,260
The tide still climbs thirty-five
metres, twice a day, and falls back.

132
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:49,275
People ask if I miss the old Moon. I do.

133
00:07:49,645 --> 00:07:53,310
But nobody who has seen this one
rise would give it back.

134
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:57,500
What would you carry up the hill
first? Tell us in the comments.
